**Polymorphism.** One interface, many behaviours. **Compile-time** (overloading): same method name, different parameters. **Run-time** (overriding): a subclass redefines a parent method and the call is resolved by the actual object type. Example: `Shape.area()` is implemented differently by `Circle` and `Rectangle`; a `List<Shape>` can call `area()` without knowing the concrete type.

**Encapsulation.** Bundling data with the methods that operate on it and **hiding the internal state** behind access control. Real life: a bank account; you cannot change the balance directly, only through `deposit` and `withdraw`, which validate the amount. Code: private fields plus public getters and setters or methods.

**Abstraction.** Exposing only **what** an object does, hiding **how** it does it, through abstract classes or interfaces. Real life: a car pedal or a TV remote; you press a button without knowing the circuits.
**Difference:** encapsulation is about protecting data (implementation level, access control); abstraction is about simplifying the design by showing essential behaviour (design level, interface).

**Inheritance.** A child class acquires fields and methods of a parent (**is-a** relationship), enabling reuse and polymorphism. Keep hierarchies shallow and prefer composition when the relation is not truly is-a.

**School example.**
```java
abstract class Person { String name; int id; abstract String role(); }
class Student extends Person { int grade; String role() { return "Student"; } }
abstract class Staff extends Person { double salary; }
class Teacher extends Staff { String subject; String role() { return "Teacher"; } }
class Clerk extends Staff { String role() { return "Clerk"; } }
```
`Person` holds common fields; `Staff` adds salary shared by employees; each subclass implements `role()` differently (polymorphism). Fields are private in real code (encapsulation), and `Person` hides how roles are computed (abstraction). Things like `Library` access can be an interface (`Borrower`) that both `Student` and `Teacher` implement instead of deeper inheritance.
